  "statement": "The business case for AI-assisted coding rests on a dangerous accounting error: treating generation time as synonymous with development velocity. While the Accelerationist perspective correctly identifies the potential for rapid prototyping, it ignores the 'Proof-of-Concept' trap where initial velocity gains are cannibalized by a 30% to 50% increase in long-term maintenance and debugging requirements. Executives must stop measuring 'lines of code produced' and start tracking 'code-to-remediation ratios' to avoid inflating their technical debt. Relying on unverified machine-generated code is not an innovation strategy; it is an offloading of labor costs from the developer to the QA and security teams, creating a hidden, compounding liability that will eventually trigger a massive, unbudgeted operational bottleneck.\n\nTreating AI integration as a 'magical' paradigm shift is a failure of management, not a technical inevitability. History shows that every major automation wave—from the transition to high-level programming languages in the 1960s to the dot-com era's rapid deployment cycles—demanded higher levels of human oversight, not less. We should integrate these tools using 'Normal Science' principles: treat AI output as a draft that requires mandatory, rigorous human audit before it ever touches a production environment. If a feature is generated by an LLM, it should be subject to a 100% manual code review threshold until the specific model version demonstrates a consistent defect rate below 0.1% in your internal testing environment.\n\nSafetyists argue that we need expensive, external adversarial verification, but this is just another layer of administrative bloat that ignores the root cause: the erosion of internal engineering rigor. You do not solve a quality problem by adding more bureaucracy; you solve it by embedding verification into the developer workflow. Direct your engineering leads to implement 'AI-Attribution' tags on all commits. By quantifying the ratio of AI-authored versus human-authored code, you can identify which teams are hiding behind synthetic productivity and which are actually maintaining software stability. This is not about existential risk; it is about protecting your margins from the 'terminal maintenance burden' of bloated, unverified, and opaque codebases.",
